    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Great Stupa began as a brick dome over relics of the Buddha under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, was enlarged to almost twice its size and cased in stone under the Shungas, and received its four gateways in the 1st century BCE and 1st century CE.</li><li>Its reliefs never show the Buddha as a human figure: he appears as a path, a footprint, an empty throne or a canopy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhi-tree">bodhi tree</a>, and local donors paid for the sculptures and had their names cut on them.</li><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> declined in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the monuments fell into disrepair; they were recorded by General Henry Taylor in 1818, ravaged by treasure hunters until 1881 and restored from 1912 to 1919 under Sir John Marshall.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Most of the Brahmi inscriptions at Sanchi are short records of gifts. In 1837 James Prinsep noticed that most of them ended with the same two characters, which he took as &quot;danam&quot; (donation), and this permitted the decipherment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

