    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">3rd century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> commissioned the Great Stupa, a brick hemisphere over relics of the Buddha</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 150 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Under the Shungas the stupa was expanded with stone slabs to almost twice its size, with a railing around it</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 115 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The first Buddhist reliefs were carved on Stupa No. 2, with mason marks in Kharoshthi</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1st century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The decorated gateways began to be built under the Satavahanas, the earliest probably under Satakarni II</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">5th century</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-ii">Chandragupta II</a>'s inscription was cut on the railing near the Eastern Gateway, and Temple 17 was built</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1818</span><span class="rf-text">General Henry Taylor recorded the site, overgrown but with several gateways still standing</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1912</span><span class="rf-text">Restoration under Sir John Marshall began and brought the structures to their present condition by 1919</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1989</span><span class="rf-text">The monuments at Sanchi were listed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sanchi Stupa is a Buddhist complex on a hilltop at Sanchi Town in Raisen District, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, about 23 kilometres from Raisen town and 46 kilometres northeast of Bhopal. Its Great Stupa, also called Stupa No. 1, was originally commissioned by the Mauryan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> in the 3rd century BCE. Its nucleus was a hemispherical brick structure built over relics of the Buddha, and the work was overseen by Ashoka, whose wife Devi was the daughter of a merchant of nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a>. Sanchi was also her birthplace and the venue of her wedding to Ashoka.</p><p>According to one version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>, the Buddhist chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, Ashoka halted at Vidisha when he was heir-apparent and journeying as Viceroy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, and there married the daughter of a local banker. She was called Devi and later gave Ashoka two sons, Ujjeniya and Mahendra, and a daughter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanghamitta">Sanghamitta</a>. After Ashoka's accession Mahendra headed a Buddhist mission to Sri Lanka, probably sent under the Emperor's auspices, and before setting out he is said to have visited his mother at Chetiyagiri near Vidisa, thought to be Sanchi, where she is said to have had a sumptuous vihara built.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bodhi-tree"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bodhi-tree.jpg" alt="Bodhi tree" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bodhi-tree">Bodhi tree</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bodhgaya_3639641913_f4c5f73689_t.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ken Wieland from Philadelphia, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Great Stupa, in its enlarged form, is a dome set on a high circular drum meant for circumambulation, reached by a double staircase. The dome was flattened near the top and crowned by three superimposed parasols within a square railing, a tiered form that was a symbol of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">dharma</a>, the Wheel of the Law. A second stone pathway at ground level is enclosed by a stone balustrade. Around the stupa stand four elabourately carved toranas, or ornamental gateways, which depict scenes from the life of the Buddha, events after his death, in particular the War of the Relics, and the efforts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> to spread the Buddhist faith.</p><p>The reliefs also illustrate Jatakas, the moral tales of the Buddha's former lives, among them the Syama, Vessantara and Mahakapi Jatakas, together with miracles and the temptation of the Buddha by Mara. In these scenes the Buddha is never represented as a human figure. His presence is shown by his path on the water, a chankrama or "promenade" slab hanging in the air, an empty throne, a footprint, a horse or a canopy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhi-tree">bodhi tree</a>. The complex also holds Stupa No. 2 and Stupa No. 3, a pillar of Ashoka with a Schism Edict, and temples from the Mauryan to the Gupta period.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/agnimitra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agnimitra.jpg" alt="Agnimitra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/agnimitra">Agnimitra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SungaMasculine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">World Imaging, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The original stupa had about half the diameter of today's and was covered in brick. On the basis of the Ashokavadana it is presumed that the stupa may have been vandalised in the 2nd century BCE, an event some have related to the rise of the Shunga ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushyamitra-shunga">Pushyamitra Shunga</a>, and it has been suggested that Pushyamitra destroyed it and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnimitra">Agnimitra</a> rebuilt it. During the Shunga period the stupa was covered with stone and the railings were added, dated to c. 150 BCE or 175–125 BCE. Some authors argue that the constructions of that period were not royal work: the dedications at Sanchi were private or collective.</p><p>The gateways were built from the 1st century BCE under the Satavahanas, who had taken eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> from the Shungas; the builder of the earliest is generally considered to be Satakarni II, who ruled in 50–25 BCE. Although made of stone, the toranas were carved and constructed in the manner of wood. An inscription on the Southern Gateway records that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a> did the carving. The reliefs at Stupa No. 2, dated to c. 115 BCE for the medallions, carry mason marks in Kharoshthi, which seems to imply workers from Gandhara in the north-west. The Ashokan pillar was carved from sandstone from the quarries of Chunar, several hundred miles away.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The local population donated money for the embellishment of the stupa to gain spiritual merit, and there was no direct royal patronage at the gateways. Men and women who paid for a sculpture often chose their favourite scene from the life of the Buddha and had their names inscribed on it, which accounts for the repetition of particular episodes. Analysis of the donation records shows that many donors were local, while others came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a>, Kurara, Nadinagar, Mahisati, Kurghara, Bhogavadhan and Kamdagigam.</p><p>Foreigners took part as well. Three inscriptions were made by self-declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yavana">Yavana</a> donors, the clearest reading "Setapathiyasa Yonasa danam" ("Gift of the Yona of Setapatha"), and some friezes show devotees in Greek-style dress. The aniconism of the reliefs may conform to an ancient prohibition on showing the Buddha's body, known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarvastivada">Sarvastivada</a> vinaya. Sanchi art is considered the ancestor of the didactic forms of Buddhist art that followed, such as that of Gandhara, which added the Buddha as a man in Greek-style clothing. More precise details of exchanges or routes of transmission between Sanchi and Gandhara are elusive.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Building continued until the 12th century CE, with Temple 45 the last Buddhist temple, built in the mid to late 9th century. With the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> the monuments went out of use and fell into disrepair. In 1818 General Henry Taylor recorded the site: the jungle had overgrown it, but the monuments were in relatively good condition and several gateways were still standing. Sanchi, on a hill, had escaped the Muslim conquerors who destroyed the nearby city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a>. Sir Herbert Maddock clumsily breached the Great Stupa in 1822 without reaching its centre. Alexander Cunningham and Frederick Charles Maisey made the first formal survey and excavations in 1851.</p><p>Amateur archaeologists and treasure hunters ravaged the site until 1881, when proper restoration began. Major Cole reconstructed two gateways in 1882–83, and between 1912 and 1919 the structures were restored under Sir John Marshall. Nineteenth-century French and English interests wished to remove the Eastern Gateway; plaster-cast copies were made instead and the original stayed in the state of Bhopal, whose rulers Shahjehan Begum and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Jehan Begum paid for preservation and a museum. A Gupta statue of Padmapani is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoria-and-albert-museum">Victoria and Albert Museum</a> and one of the Yashinis in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bharhut"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bharhut.jpg" alt="Bharhut" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bharhut">Bharhut</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bharhut_stupa_original_layout.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cunningham, Alexander, Sir, 1814-1893, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sanchi encompasses most of the evolutions of ancient Indian and Buddhist architecture, from the early stages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and its first artistic expression to the decline of the religion in the subcontinent. The Great Stupa is one of the oldest stone structures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and about fifty monuments remain on the hill, including three main stupas and several temples. The reliefs, especially those depicting Indian cities, have been important in trying to imagine what ancient Indian cities looked like, and many modern simulations are based on them. The Sanchi pillar capital also belongs to a line of capitals running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a> capital through the pillar capitals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bharhut">Bharhut</a>.</p><p>The relics found by Maisey and Cunningham were divided and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and Maisey's family sold the objects to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoria-and-albert-museum">Victoria and Albert Museum</a>. Buddhists in England, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and India, led by the Mahabodhi Society, demanded their return. Some relics of Sariputta and Moggallana were sent to Sri Lanka and shown in 1947, and the Chetiyagiri Vihara was built in 1952 to house them, an event that in a nationalistic sense marked the formal re-establishment of the Buddhist tradition in India. The monuments have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 1989.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sanchi is a hilltop Buddhist complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> whose Great Stupa, commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, was enlarged under the Shungas and given four carved gateways under the Satavahanas. It fell into disuse, was recorded in 1818 and restored in the early 20th century.</em></p>

